    "id": "WE-04",
    "category": "Workflow Engineering",
    "title": "04. Document Ingestion Pipelines & The Ingestion Lifecycle",
    "tags": [
      "Document Ingestion",
      "Content Hashing",
      "ETL",
      "PDF Parsing",
      "Caching"
    ],
    "summary": "Explore the 4-step document ingestion lifecycle, why content hash verification saves 95%+ of compute costs, and master unstructured data parsing.",
    "content": "### 1. What is Document Ingestion? (The Definitive Definition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Formal Technical Definition (Exam Standard):**  \n> **Document Ingestion** is the automated extract-transform-load (ETL) pipeline that parses heterogeneous unstructured and semi-structured documents (PDFs, Word files, HTML, scanned images, tabular data), extracts text and metadata, segments content into semantically coherent chunks, computes dense vector embeddings, and indexes them into a retrieval store for semantic search.\n\n#### 📚 The Encyclopedia & Recipe Card Analogy\nImagine trying to read a 1,000-page encyclopedia to answer: *\"How much salt goes into a French onion soup?\"*\n\nYou cannot feed an entire 1,000-page book into your eyes at once. Instead, a librarian:\n1. Opens the book and extracts the text (**Step 1: Extract**).\n2. Checks if they already photocopied this page yesterday (**Step 2: Content Hash / Cache Check**).\n3. Cuts the text into small, bite-sized recipe index cards (**Step 3: Chunking**).\n4. Tags each card with keywords and files them into the catalog cabinet (**Step 4: Embed & Store**).\n\n```text\nTHE 4-STEP INGESTION LIFECYCLE\n┌──────────────────┐     ┌───────────────────────┐     ┌───────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────┐\n│  Step 1: Extract │ ──► │ Step 2: Content Hash  │ ──► │  Step 3: Chunk    │ ──► │ Step 4: Embed & Store│\n│  Parse raw files │     │ Check docstore cache  │     │ 300-500 tokens    │     │ Vectorize and index  │\n│  OCR & metadata  │     │ (Skip unchanged docs) │     │ with 10-20% overlap│    │ in Vector DB         │\n└──────────────────┘     └───────────────────────┘     └───────────────────┘     └──────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The #1 Exam Question — Content Hash Verification (Cache Check)\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **High-Yield Exam Trap:** What is the primary purpose of Step 2 (Content Hash Verification)?\n>\n> In enterprise companies (banks, hospitals, law firms), **over 95% of documents do NOT change** between daily ingestion runs!\n>\n> If you re-chunk and re-embed 100,000 unchanged PDFs every night:\n> * You burn thousands of dollars in unnecessary embedding API costs.\n> * You waste hours of GPU compute time.\n> * You lock your vector database with millions of redundant write operations.\n\n#### How Content Hashing Works in 3 Simple Steps:\nWhen a document is read, calculate its fingerprint:\n\n$$\\text{hash} = \\text{SHA-256}(\\text{raw\\_text})$$\n\n1. Look up the database to see if `document_id` already has that exact hash.\n2. **If matches (`CACHE_HIT`):** Skip immediately! Zero API cost, zero wasted time.\n3. **If different (`CACHE_MISS`):** The file was edited. Delete existing vectors for that `doc_id` and re-process.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Handling Different Document Formats\n\nReal-world enterprise documents come in many messy formats. Here is the standard parser matrix:\n\n| Document Format | Primary Real-World Challenge | Recommended Industry Parser |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Digital PDF** | Two-column academic layouts, running headers/footers. | `pdfplumber`, `PyPDF`, `UnstructuredPDFLoader` |\n| **Scanned PDF (Image)** | Pixel noise, skewed camera angles, no selectable text. | OCR Engines: `Tesseract OCR`, AWS Textract |\n| **Markdown / Code** | Preserving indentation, tables, and function boundaries. | AST Parsers, `MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter` |\n| **HTML / Web Pages** | Navigation bars, cookie consent banners, scripts. | `trafilatura` (removes boilerplate ads/menus) |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Production Implementation (Python)\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom typing import Dict\nfrom langchain_core.documents import Document\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\nclass ProductionIngestionPipeline:\n    def __init__(self, vector_store, cache_store: Dict[str, str]):\n        self.vector_store = vector_store\n        self.cache_store = cache_store  # Maps doc_id -> sha256_hash\n        self.splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=500, chunk_overlap=50)\n\n    def get_sha256(self, text: str) -> str:\n        return hashlib.sha256(text.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\n\n    def ingest(self, doc_id: str, raw_content: str, metadata: dict) -> str:\n        # Step 1 & 2: Extract & Check Hash\n        current_hash = self.get_sha256(raw_content)\n        if self.cache_store.get(doc_id) == current_hash:\n            return f\"[CACHE HIT] Document {doc_id} unchanged. Skipped re-embedding!\"\n\n        # Handle Cache Miss: Delete old vectors if document was previously indexed\n        if doc_id in self.cache_store:\n            try:\n                self.vector_store.delete(filter={\"doc_id\": doc_id})\n            except (NotImplementedError, AttributeError):\n                pass  # Handles stores where metadata-filtered delete is configured differently\n\n        # Step 3: Chunking with Overlap & Metadata Attachment\n        doc = Document(\n            page_content=raw_content, \n            metadata={**metadata, \"doc_id\": doc_id, \"hash\": current_hash}\n        )\n        chunks = self.splitter.split_documents([doc])\n\n        # Step 4: Embed & Store\n        self.vector_store.add_documents(chunks)\n        self.cache_store[doc_id] = current_hash\n        return f\"[PROCESSED] Successfully indexed {len(chunks)} chunks for {doc_id}.\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ 2-Minute Exam Quick Review Cheat Sheet\n\n* **4 Steps in Order:** Extract → Content Hash (Cache Check) → Chunk → Embed & Store.\n* **Why Content Hash?** Avoids re-embedding the 95%+ of documents that never changed. Saves compute, API costs, and vector DB write locks.\n* **Metadata Rule:** Always attach `doc_id`, `created_at`, `source_url`, and `access_tier` to each chunk to enable permission and tenant filtering at query time.\n* **Dead Letter Queue (DLQ):** Corrupted, empty, or password-protected files must be routed to a DLQ for offline inspection rather than terminating the pipeline batch job."
