    "summary": "Compare in-process local Python tools vs out-of-process MCP servers across latency, failure isolation, universal reusability, and Docker container sandboxing.",
    "content": "### 1. What are Local Tools vs. MCP Servers? (The Definitive Definition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Formal Technical Definition (Exam Standard):**\n> **Local Tools** are in-process callable Python functions executed directly within the host agent's runtime memory space, maximizing performance at the cost of coupling. **MCP Servers** are decoupled, out-of-process services communicating over standardized JSON-RPC protocols, providing universal reusability, language independence, and isolated security boundaries at the cost of minor serialization latency.\n\n#### 🔌 In-Memory vs. Network Cable Analogy (Explain It Simply)\n* **Local Tools (In-Process):** Like a muscle in your arm. Your brain sends a signal directly through a nerve, and the muscle moves instantly in microseconds.\n* **MCP Tools (Out-of-Process):** Like a remote control drone. Your brain sends a radio signal to the drone, the drone flies, and radios the video back. It has minor radio latency, but the drone can be swapped out, upgraded, or flown by someone else without modifying your body!\n\n---\n\n### 2. In-Process Local Tools vs. Out-of-Process MCP Tools: Architectural Trade-offs\n\n| Architectural Feature | In-Process Local Tools (Python Functions) | Out-of-Process MCP Tools (Protocol Servers) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Execution Boundary** | Runs directly inside the agent's Python memory space. | Runs as an independent process (local subprocess or remote container). |\n| **Invocation Latency** | **Sub-millisecond (<0.1ms).** Pure Python function call. | **Minor overhead (2–10ms).** JSON-RPC serialization over stdio / HTTP. |\n| **Reusability** | **Locked to Python.** Cannot be used by Claude Desktop, Cursor, or Node.js apps. | **Universal.** Can be plugged into Claude, Cursor, or any MCP-compatible agent. |\n| **Failure Isolation** | **None.** If the tool has a segmentation fault or crashes, the entire agent dies! | **Total.** If the server crashes, the host stays alive and handles the error cleanly. |\n| **Security Sandboxing** | Difficult. Runs with full host process permissions. | **High.** Can be isolated inside dedicated Docker containers or limited users. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. When to Build Local Python Tools vs. When to Build / Consume MCP\n\n* **Build Local Tools When:**\n  * Performance and latency are paramount (e.g. tight math loops, internal vector searches).\n  * The tool requires complex non-serializable in-memory objects (e.g. raw PyTorch tensors, open DB connections).\n  * The tool is tightly coupled to a single specific agent application and will never be shared.\n* **Build / Consume MCP When:**\n  * You want your tools to work across multiple AI platforms (Claude Desktop, Cursor, LangGraph).\n  * The tool is written in another language (Go, Node.js, Rust).\n  * The tool performs dangerous or privileged operations requiring sandboxed container isolation.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Security Boundaries: Running MCP Servers in Isolated Environments (Containers)\n\nRunning tools out-of-process enables true enterprise security isolation:\n* Run the MCP server inside a minimal **Docker container** with read-only filesystems.\n* Restrict container network access using firewall rules so compromised tools cannot exfiltrate internal data.\n* If an attacker uses Prompt Injection to make the agent execute a malicious script, the damage is 100% trapped inside the container!\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ 2-Minute Exam Quick Review Cheat Sheet\n\n* **Local Tools:** In-process, ultra-low latency (<0.1ms), tightly coupled to Python runtime.\n* **MCP Servers:** Out-of-process, JSON-RPC protocol, language-agnostic, universally reusable.\n* **Failure Blast Radius:** MCP crashes do not crash the agent; Local tool crashes terminate the host process.\n* **Container Isolation:** MCP servers can run inside locked-down Docker containers for secure sandboxing."
